= S2.7 Within-child direct-age metadata sensitivity
As a robustness check of S2.5, the primary 3D within-child family was re-run on the 36 visits from 18 children with spreadsheet-direct ages (`direct_age_metadata_sensitivity.csv`). No endpoint crossed nominal significance: the smallest raw p was `rotation_norm_median` at 0.208 (FDR sensitivity p=0.636). This is a materially different result from the pre-masking export, where the same two endpoints (`frobenius_norm_median`, raw p=0.050; `rotation_norm_median`, raw p=0.043) crossed nominal significance before FDR correction; both are now well above 0.2 (`frobenius_norm_median` raw p=0.277). Because artifact-flagged segments were specifically masked between the two exports, this pattern is consistent with the earlier nominal hits having been driven by residual motion artifact rather than a developmental effect, though this inference is descriptive (a before/after comparison across two non-independent exports of the same recordings), not a formal statistical test. It is reported here in full for completeness alongside the cohort-coordinate direct-age sensitivity (S2.4).

= S2.8 Cohort-pooled reachability family (robustness check)
Results are stored in `results/figshare_infant_cohort_20260805_132612/05_cohort_pooled_reachability/`. All 70 visits were testable (`cohort_pooled_reachability_per_visit.csv`), drawing from a pooled set of 5,195 cross-session epoch-to-epoch transitions (down from 6,851 pre-masking) with a median of 8 distinct contributing children per visit after leave-one-child-out exclusion. None of the five endpoints was FDR-supported or nominally significant in the primary within-child-style test (`cohort_pooled_reachability_age_associations.csv`): rotational proxy (standardized slope $-0.25$, raw p=0.196, the smallest raw p in this family), directional entropy (raw p=0.277), effective dimension (raw p=0.351), log-volume (raw p=0.411), and anisotropy (raw p=0.478). This is a cleaner primary result than the pre-masking export, whose smallest raw p (anisotropy, 0.035) was nominally significant though not FDR-supported. The direct-age-only subset (36 visits/18 children, `cohort_pooled_reachability_direct_age_sensitivity.csv`) showed one nominal (uncorrected) hit -- rotational proxy (standardized slope $-0.47$, raw p=0.040, FDR sensitivity p=0.200) -- not FDR-supported. The per-visit neighbor $k$-th-distance was stable across age terciles (medians 0.352, 0.292, 0.329 in the youngest, middle, and oldest thirds), so these nulls are not attributable to a strong age-density confound in the pooled neighbor search. This is a distinct local-dynamics estimand from both the within-child family (S2.5-S2.7, estimated from each session's own epochs) and the cohort-coordinate GEE (S2.1, chart position rather than local flow).

= S2.9 Result files
`first_to_last_change.csv` gives descriptive first-to-last within-child changes and age spans. It is not a shared-timepoint contrast because children entered and exited the study at different ages. Session-level source data and analysis manifests provide the complete auditable numeric record.

= S2.10 Conventional-EEG (qEEG) comparator family: cohort-level GEE
Results are stored in `results/figshare_infant_cohort_20260805_132612/06_conventional_eeg_age/conventional_eeg_cohort_gee_results.csv` (S1.8; see that supplement for the second-export provenance note). Eighteen of 34 endpoints (all non-connectivity comparators) have 100% session coverage; the 16 connectivity endpoints have 80--96% coverage (S2.12). In the cohort-level spline GEE, 24 of 34 endpoints are FDR-supported: 14 of 18 non-connectivity endpoints (the four exceptions are the alpha/theta ratio, delta/alpha ratio, slowing index, and theta/alpha ratio) and 10 of 16 connectivity endpoints. Among the fully-covered endpoints, the strongest are the beta/alpha ratio ($chi^2=201.8$, FDR p=$1.94 times 10^-42$) and relative beta power ($chi^2=75.7$, FDR p=$1.49 times 10^-15$). Among the connectivity endpoints, the alpha frontal-central coherence mean and SD ($chi^2=297.1$ and $434.1$, FDR p=$7.09 times 10^-63$ and $3.05 times 10^-92$) and theta frontal-central coherence mean and SD ($chi^2=156.7$ and $93.4$, FDR p=$7.91 times 10^-33$ and $2.79 times 10^-19$) are, by a wide margin, the largest cohort-level test statistics in this entire study; the alpha frontal-central pair is specifically flagged for a possible non-neural confound in the main text (Results R6, Discussion).

= S2.11 Conventional-EEG within/between decomposition and cluster-permutation check
Results are stored in `conventional_eeg_within_between_decomposition.csv` and `conventional_eeg_cohort_gee_cluster_permutation.csv` (identical M3/M4 estimators as S2.2-S2.3, applied to this family, 500 permutations/endpoint, seed 42). The within-child spline term is FDR-supported at 31 of 34 endpoints; the between-child linear term is FDR-supported at only 1 of 34 (theta F8-P4 coherence SD, between raw p=$4.13 times 10^-5$), so -- as in the primary cohort-coordinate family (S2.2) -- this family's cohort-level effect is overwhelmingly within-child rather than a between-child/cross-sectional artifact, including for the two most extreme frontal-central coherence endpoints (within raw p=$1.04 times 10^-27$ and $8.18 times 10^-7$; between raw p=0.22 and 0.16). The child-cluster row-permutation check is materially more conservative, as in S2.3: only 5 of 34 endpoints survive its own FDR correction -- alpha frontal-central coherence mean and SD (permutation FDR p=0.0226 each), the beta/alpha ratio (0.0226), and relative beta and gamma power (0.0271 each). This is a comparably steep reduction from the asymptotic family (24/34 to 5/34) as seen for the primary cohort-coordinate family (12/12 to 6/12, S2.3), while endpoint count and the 500-permutation resolution also differ between families.

= S2.12 Conventional-EEG within-child age associations and comparator coverage
Results are stored in `conventional_eeg_within_child_age_associations.csv` and `conventional_eeg_within_child_direct_age_sensitivity.csv` (identical S1.3 within-child estimator used for R4/R5, applied to this family; 68 endpoint-complete visits from 20 children primary, 36 visits from 18 children direct-age-only). Six of 34 endpoints are FDR-supported: permutation entropy (FDR sensitivity p=0.036), the beta/alpha ratio (0.007), the slowing index (0.044), relative beta power (0.034), relative gamma power (0.034), and theta frontal-central coherence mean (0.044). The spectral endpoint directions are consistent with prior reports of infant EEG spectral maturation @xiao2018power @marshall2002eeg @schaworonkow2021aperiodic; complexity context is available from infant EEG work on multiscale entropy, including a preterm cohort that is not directly comparable to this sample @pelc2022mse. The theta coherence result is reported without an expected-direction interpretation because it belongs to the lower-coverage connectivity pack and is not supported by the cluster-permutation check. The beta/alpha ratio and relative beta/gamma power are the only endpoints supported by *both* this check and the cluster-permutation check (S2.11), making them the most robust conventional-EEG endpoints overall. Comparator coverage (`conventional_eeg_coverage.csv`) is 100% (70/70 sessions) for all 18 non-connectivity endpoints and 95.7% (F4-P4 pair), 81.4% (F8-P4 pair), or 80.0% (central-parietal and frontal-central pairs) for the 16 connectivity endpoints; endpoints below the family's minimum-session/minimum-distinct-age/minimum-within-child-variation thresholds (S1.8) are marked `not_testable` rather than silently dropped or causing a fit failure, though no connectivity endpoint fell below those thresholds in this export.

